<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 1950s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi&quot;, or &quot;Tūtira Mai&quot;, is a Māori folk song (waiata) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, written in the late 1950s by Canon Wiremu Te Tau Huata, a military chaplain to the 28th Māori Battalion, while he was part of an Ecumenical Movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Huata wrote the song while driving from Wairoa, Hawke's Bay, to Napier with his children: passing Lake Tūtira, he sang the words and the children repeated them, learning it on the way. It spread as he performed it in churches and Bible classes. By the 1960s the Ministry of Education was publishing it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> schools without his consent and without crediting him, and after fifty years of use in schools his daughter revealed that some of the words had been printed incorrectly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>As with many older folk songs, there is some conjecture about the exact words, but it is generally agreed to be a song of unity, with the repeated refrain tātou tātou e, meaning all of us, all of us. New Zealanders often sing it at times when standing together and supporting one another is fitting; it was used, for example, to show support for the New Zealand Muslim community after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch-mosque-shootings">Christchurch mosque shootings</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 2017 the New Zealand Rugby Union, in a campaign led by the police constable and former All Black Glen Osborne, urged fans to adopt the song as a rallying chant during the British and Irish Lions tour. The attempt was poorly received by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-blacks">All Blacks</a> fans, the Lions supporters changed the chorus to Lions, Lions, and a New Zealand sports writer judged it an inappropriate choice.</p><p>On 28 May 2020 a performance by navy, army and air force bands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was published online, with the permission of Huata's whānau. On 12 November 2022, at Eden Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, Ruby Tui of the Black Ferns led the crowd in singing it to celebrate the team's 34-31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> final win over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi</b>&quot;, or &quot;Tūtira Mai&quot;, is a Māori folk song (waiata) of New Zealand, written in the late 1950s by Canon Wiremu Te Tau Huata, a military chaplain to the 28th Māori Battalion, while he was part of an Ecumenical Movement.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tutira-mai-nga-iwi">Tūtira Mai Ngā Iwi</a></li></ul></section>
